, "baseline_margin": 13.94, "analyst_adjustment": 0.06, "key_factors": ["Unanimous Safe/Solid D expert ratings and markets pricing Pritzker at 97-98%", "Incumbent Pritzker's heavy spending in a state leaning Democratic by six points", "Trump's September endorsement of Bailey nationalizes the race in a blue state", "Thin polling: a single November 2025 survey showed Pritzker ahead 54-34"], "rationale": "Every major rater places the race in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, prediction markets price Pritzker near 97-98%, and the lone poll shows him up twenty points; Trump's endorsement of Bailey is unlikely to overcome Illinois' Democratic lean and Pritzker's financial edge.", "watch": "Fresh general-election polling or evidence that the Bailey endorsement is shifting downstate or suburban turnout could move the margin.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent JB Pritzker (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor JB Pritzker seeks re-election against Republican Darren Bailey, whom President Trump endorsed in mid-September. Illinois' six-point Democratic lean, Pritzker's incumbency, and his heavy spending frame the contest. Every major race rating—Cook, Sabato's, Fox News, RealClearPolitics, Silver Bulletin, and Decision Desk—places it in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and prediction markets price a Pritzker win near 97 to 98 percent.\n\nThe only available poll, from November 2025, showed Pritzker ahead 54 to 34 among likely voters, though polling in the race remains sparse. Bailey's path runs through nationalizing the election and turning out Trump-aligned voters, while Pritzker's national profile and spending dominate the airwaves. Pritzker is expected to win by roughly fourteen points, with residual uncertainty reflecting the thin public polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.98, "margin": 13.88}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9829, "margin": 14.